diagnosis_text:
Affected component: ISP-A’s routing (default route) on interface eth0. 

Mechanism: End hosts (e.g., PC1 at 10.1.0.100) have correct LAN routing and reach the distribution/core and R1; R1 is NATing correctly and forwards internet-bound traffic to ISP-A (default via 203.0.113.1 on r1 eth2). However, ISP-A’s default route points back to the customer (default via 203.0.113.2 on ispa eth0). This creates a routing loop for any destination not covered by more-specific routes: r1 → ispa → r1 … until TTL expires, which appears to users as web pages “spinning” and timing out.

Localization: 
- PC1: default via 10.1.0.1 and can ping 10.1.0.1 and r1 (evidence: pc1 ping to 10.1.0.1 OK; ping pc1→r1 OK). 
- R1: default via 203.0.113
