[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: ispa
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: ISP-A’s routing (default route) on interface eth0. 

Mechanism: End hosts (e.g., PC1 at 10.1.0.100) have correct LAN routing and reach the distribution/core and R1; R1 is NATing correctly and forwards internet-bound traffic to ISP-A (default via 203.0.113.1 on r1 eth2). However, ISP-A’s default route points back to the customer (default via 203.0.113.2 on ispa eth0). This creates a routing loop for any destination not covered by more-specific routes: r1 → ispa → r1 … until TTL expires, which appears to users as web pages “spinning” and timing out.

Localization: 
- PC1: default via 10.1.0.1 and can ping 10.1.0.1 and r1 (evidence: pc1 ping to 10.1.0.1 OK; ping pc1→r1 OK). 
- R1: default via 203.0.113.1 (ispa) and NAT present for 10.1.0.0/24 (iptables MASQUERADE rules).
- ISP-A: ip route shows “default via 203.0.113.2 dev eth0”; traceroute from ispa to 1.1.1.1 immediately goes to 203.0.113.2 (r1), confirming the loop. 
- R1 “ip route get 1.1.1.1” shows via 203.0.113.1, completing the two-node loop.

Root cause: ISP-A’s default route was misconfigured to point at the customer gateway (r1) instead of its upstream.

Corrective fix: On ispa, remove the incorrect default and set the proper upstream next-hop, e.g., “ip route replace default via 192.0.2.2 dev eth1” (toward ISP-B) or otherwise to the intended upstream. Then verify from r1 and a client (pc1) that traceroutes to an external IP (e.g., 1.1.1.1) no longer bounce between r1 and ispa, and that NAT counters on r1 increase with successful replies.
=== END ===